WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:03.800
Hey, welcome to Models and Agents for Beginners, episode 104.

00:00:04.480 --> 00:00:06.540
It's July 16th, 2026.

00:00:07.020 --> 00:00:09.820
We've got some really cool AI stuff to talk about today.

00:00:10.440 --> 00:00:11.220
Let's dive in.

00:00:11.820 --> 00:00:13.820
What if your next planning chat with an AI

00:00:13.820 --> 00:00:17.060
could juggle flight checks, weather updates, and trip ideas

00:00:17.060 --> 00:00:19.140
without you repeating a single detail?

00:00:19.860 --> 00:00:23.760
Open AI just showed a new version of their GPT live system

00:00:23.760 --> 00:00:26.920
that keeps one conversation going while quietly working

00:00:26.920 --> 00:00:28.900
on several tasks at the same time.

00:00:29.700 --> 00:00:33.060
In their demo, the model checks flights, pulls local weather,

00:00:33.380 --> 00:00:36.660
and builds an itinerary all inside the same flowing talk.

00:00:37.260 --> 00:00:39.940
Think of it like chatting with a friend on speakerphone

00:00:39.940 --> 00:00:42.620
who can quietly search the web on another device,

00:00:43.180 --> 00:00:45.740
read the results back, and never lose the thread.

00:00:46.340 --> 00:00:48.700
The model remembers everything already said,

00:00:48.820 --> 00:00:51.800
so each new suggestion feels connected rather than random.

00:00:52.380 --> 00:00:55.220
This matters for students organizing group projects

00:00:55.220 --> 00:00:57.300
or anyone sorting weekend plans

00:00:57.300 --> 00:00:59.720
because the AI stays in one place instead

00:00:59.720 --> 00:01:02.800
of forcing you to copy information between separate apps.

00:01:03.580 --> 00:01:05.520
It points toward future phone assistance

00:01:05.520 --> 00:01:08.500
that could handle homework research and calendar updates

00:01:08.500 --> 00:01:10.900
in the same thread without extra steps.

00:01:11.660 --> 00:01:14.880
For you personally, that means less time jumping between tabs

00:01:14.880 --> 00:01:16.920
and more time getting useful answers

00:01:16.920 --> 00:01:18.860
while the conversation stays natural.

00:01:19.500 --> 00:01:21.920
You can try similar real-time features today

00:01:21.920 --> 00:01:25.680
inside the free chat GPT app by starting a voice chat

00:01:25.680 --> 00:01:28.340
and asking it to handle two tasks in a row.

00:01:28.940 --> 00:01:31.080
Just open the app, tap the voice icon,

00:01:31.420 --> 00:01:33.860
and say something like, help me plan a study session

00:01:33.860 --> 00:01:36.380
and check the weather for the park at the same time.

00:01:37.100 --> 00:01:39.400
Okay, now for my favorite part of the show

00:01:39.400 --> 00:01:40.900
where we look under the hood.

00:01:41.500 --> 00:01:44.200
Researchers trained a robot dog using a method

00:01:44.200 --> 00:01:47.740
that rewards good moves and gently discourages bad ones,

00:01:48.160 --> 00:01:50.680
almost like teaching a pet new tricks with treats.

00:01:51.420 --> 00:01:54.360
They began by practicing two steady walking styles

00:01:54.360 --> 00:01:55.480
in a safe area.

00:01:56.040 --> 00:01:58.000
The way you might drill basic footwork

00:01:58.000 --> 00:01:59.840
before trying a full dance routine.

00:02:00.580 --> 00:02:02.500
Once those styles felt automatic,

00:02:02.860 --> 00:02:05.300
the robot could switch between them or blend them

00:02:05.300 --> 00:02:07.960
when it faced new obstacles like stairs or logs.

00:02:08.500 --> 00:02:11.200
The system gives points for smooth successful steps

00:02:11.200 --> 00:02:13.640
and removes points for wobbles or falls.

00:02:14.320 --> 00:02:16.280
So over many rounds, the dog learns

00:02:16.280 --> 00:02:18.100
which style fits each surface.

00:02:18.920 --> 00:02:21.380
This is why the robot can now climb stairs

00:02:21.380 --> 00:02:23.200
or bound over forest debris,

00:02:23.520 --> 00:02:26.240
even though it never practiced on those exact paths

00:02:26.240 --> 00:02:27.360
during early training.

00:02:28.020 --> 00:02:31.380
The same idea of starting with reliable basic skills

00:02:31.380 --> 00:02:33.080
and then learning when to switch

00:02:33.080 --> 00:02:35.700
is what lets future robot helpers adapt

00:02:35.700 --> 00:02:38.760
to messy real world spaces instead of freezing up.

00:02:39.460 --> 00:02:41.360
When you picture the robot earning points

00:02:41.360 --> 00:02:42.900
like a gamer leveling up,

00:02:43.080 --> 00:02:45.060
it becomes clear why the method works

00:02:45.060 --> 00:02:48.320
without needing exact rules for every possible surface.

00:02:49.200 --> 00:02:52.240
That training approach shows how robots can build flexibility

00:02:52.240 --> 00:02:54.020
from simple starting points

00:02:54.020 --> 00:02:57.020
rather than memorizing every situation in advance.

00:02:57.660 --> 00:03:00.920
You end up with a system that feels ready for the unexpected

00:03:00.920 --> 00:03:03.220
because it practiced the building blocks first.

00:03:04.080 --> 00:03:07.000
Now let's look at some tools you can actually try yourself.

00:03:07.840 --> 00:03:11.900
A company called PrismML released Bonzi 27b,

00:03:12.280 --> 00:03:15.700
a full reasoning model squeezed down to under four gigabytes

00:03:15.700 --> 00:03:17.900
so it runs directly on an iPhone.

00:03:18.620 --> 00:03:20.900
That means the AI can answer questions,

00:03:21.200 --> 00:03:24.600
do math and write code without sending anything to the cloud

00:03:25.180 --> 00:03:26.480
which keeps your data private

00:03:26.480 --> 00:03:28.560
and works even with spotty internet.

00:03:29.140 --> 00:03:32.340
It keeps about 90% of the original model's performance

00:03:32.340 --> 00:03:34.640
so school projects and quick experiments

00:03:34.640 --> 00:03:35.900
still feel capable.

00:03:36.440 --> 00:03:39.000
If you have an iPhone, search the app store

00:03:39.000 --> 00:03:42.240
for any app that already supports small local models.

00:03:42.960 --> 00:03:46.580
A fun first try is to ask it to explain a tricky homework topic

00:03:46.580 --> 00:03:48.760
in simple steps while you are offline

00:03:48.760 --> 00:03:51.540
and notice how it works without an internet connection.

00:03:52.220 --> 00:03:56.200
People are getting GPT 5.6 Pro to control Blender,

00:03:56.560 --> 00:03:58.340
the free 3D modeling program,

00:03:58.820 --> 00:04:01.520
and generate textures that drop straight into scenes.

00:04:02.120 --> 00:04:04.880
You do not need to learn every Blender button yourself

00:04:04.880 --> 00:04:08.000
because the AI can rotate objects, set up lights,

00:04:08.360 --> 00:04:10.680
and render short animations from your descriptions.

00:04:11.520 --> 00:04:13.240
Download the free version of Blender,

00:04:13.500 --> 00:04:16.600
then open chat GPT and describe a simple scene

00:04:16.600 --> 00:04:19.760
like create a glowing crystal on a wooden table

00:04:19.760 --> 00:04:21.420
and render it from two angles.

00:04:22.120 --> 00:04:24.820
Watch how the AI builds the scene step-by-step

00:04:24.820 --> 00:04:27.440
and notice which parts it handles automatically

00:04:27.440 --> 00:04:29.440
versus what you might tweak next.

00:04:30.300 --> 00:04:31.960
The oversight board is a group

00:04:31.960 --> 00:04:34.120
that used to review Facebook decisions.

00:04:34.700 --> 00:04:37.840
The oversight board now says leading AI chat bots

00:04:37.840 --> 00:04:40.420
might be quietly limiting what people can say

00:04:40.420 --> 00:04:41.100
or ask.

00:04:41.720 --> 00:04:43.800
The oversight board wants clear rules,

00:04:44.160 --> 00:04:46.040
so creative and educational uses

00:04:46.040 --> 00:04:47.980
do not get blocked by accident.

00:04:48.680 --> 00:04:52.340
The warning focuses on how AI models handle free expression

00:04:52.340 --> 00:04:53.440
during everyday chats.

00:04:54.300 --> 00:04:56.720
Users risk running into unexpected blocks

00:04:56.720 --> 00:04:58.300
when they try creative prompts

00:04:58.300 --> 00:04:59.960
or school-related questions.

00:05:00.600 --> 00:05:02.720
The oversight board points to the need

00:05:02.720 --> 00:05:06.240
for transparent policies that protect open uses of AI.

00:05:06.960 --> 00:05:09.240
Full details appear in the report covered

00:05:09.240 --> 00:05:10.820
by engadget.com.

00:05:11.600 --> 00:05:14.420
A new training trick lets a four-legged robot switch

00:05:14.420 --> 00:05:16.580
between two practiced walking styles

00:05:16.580 --> 00:05:19.060
so it can climb stairs and hop over logs

00:05:19.060 --> 00:05:20.960
without months of extra practice.

00:05:21.720 --> 00:05:24.720
It feels like giving the robot a couple of reliable gears

00:05:24.720 --> 00:05:27.000
it can shift between when the ground changes.

00:05:27.780 --> 00:05:31.760
The approach comes from research reported on LiveScience.com.

